var e={id:`transition`,objectives:[`Define a transition element in terms of d-block and partially-filled d subshell`,`Write electronic configurations of first-row transition elements and their ions`,`Compare physical properties of transition metals with calcium (typical s-block)`,`Explain variable oxidation states in terms of 3d/4s energy similarity`,`Describe complex ions: coordinate bonds, shapes (octahedral, tetrahedral, square planar, linear)`,`Identify types of isomerism in complexes (cis/trans, optical)`,`Explain ligand exchange and stability constants; apply the chelate effect`,`Explain the origin of colour in transition complexes (d-orbital splitting)`,`Relate magnetic properties to unpaired d electrons`,`Describe the chemistry, occurrence, and uses of V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu`,`Describe the extraction and importance of platinum group metals`],chapters:[{id:`t1`,title:`What Defines a Transition Element`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`A d-block element is any element whose highest-energy electrons go into a d orbital. Not every d-block element is a transition element though — the syllabus-level definition is sharper.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Transition element`,body:`A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a partially-filled d subshell.`},{type:`h`,text:`The exclusions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Scandium (Sc) — only forms Sc³⁺, which has a 3d⁰ configuration. No partially-filled d. Not a true TM.`,`Zinc (Zn) — only forms Zn²⁺, which is 3d¹⁰. Fully-filled d subshell, not partially filled. Not a true TM.`,`Sc and Zn are d-block but not transition elements. A common exam trap.`]},{type:`h`,text:`The four characteristic properties`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Variable oxidation states (e.g. Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺, or Mn going +2, +4, +6, +7).`,`Form coloured ions and compounds.`,`Show catalytic activity (as elements and as ions).`,`Form complex ions with ligands.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`These properties come from one source`,body:`All four arise because transition elements have an incomplete d subshell in the ion. It's the partially-filled d electrons that give variable oxidation states, the d-d transitions that give colour, the empty d orbitals that allow ligand binding, and the variable oxidation states that enable catalysis.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Transition metal = d-block + at least one ion with partly filled d.`,`Sc and Zn fail the test — they're d-block but NOT transition elements.`,`Four characteristic behaviours all trace back to partially-filled d orbitals.`]}]},{id:`t2`,title:`Electronic Configurations`,minutes:12,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Across the first-row transition series (Sc to Zn), the 3d subshell is being progressively filled. Two elements break the smooth pattern because half-filled and fully-filled d subshells are extra stable.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`ELECTRONIC CONFIGURATIONS · FIRST ROW`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Element`,`Configuration`,`Note`],rows:[[`Sc`,`[Ar] 3d¹ 4s²`,``],[`Ti`,`[Ar] 3d² 4s²`,``],[`V`,`[Ar] 3d³ 4s²`,``],[`Cr`,`[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹`,`anomaly`],[`Mn`,`[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s²`,``],[`Fe`,`[Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²`,``],[`Co`,`[Ar] 3d⁷ 4s²`,``],[`Ni`,`[Ar] 3d⁸ 4s²`,``],[`Cu`,`[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹`,`anomaly`],[`Zn`,`[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s²`,``]]},{type:`note`,text:`Cr and Cu gain stability from half-filled / filled d subshells.`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`Sc and Zn are not typical transition metals because their common stable ions lack partially-filled d subshells.`}]},caption:`Configurations across the first row`},{type:`h`,text:`Forming ions — 4s leaves first`},{type:`p`,text:`Even though 4s fills before 3d in the neutral atom (because 4s is slightly lower in energy), the 4s electrons leave FIRST when the atom is ionised. This is because once 3d starts filling, the 3d electrons actually become lower in energy than 4s.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Fe:    [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²      →    Fe²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁶      (remove both 4s)`,`Fe²⁺:  [Ar] 3d⁶          →    Fe³⁺: [Ar] 3d⁵      (then remove one 3d)`,`Cu:    [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹     →    Cu⁺:  [Ar] 3d¹⁰      (remove one 4s)`,`Cu⁺:   [Ar] 3d¹⁰         →    Cu²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁹      (then remove one 3d)`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Cr and Cu anomalies`,body:`Cr is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ (not 3d⁴ 4s²) because a half-filled d subshell is unusually stable. Cu is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (not 3d⁹ 4s²) because a fully-filled d subshell is unusually stable.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`4s fills first, but 4s empties first — write configurations accordingly.`,`Cr (3d⁵ 4s¹) and Cu (3d¹⁰ 4s¹) are anomalies — memorise these.`,`Common transition ions: Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺ to Mn⁷⁺, Cr³⁺/Cr⁶⁺.`]}]},{id:`t3`,title:`Physical Properties vs Calcium`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Transition metals are dense, hard, high-melting, and good conductors. Compared to calcium (a typical s-block metal sitting immediately before the TM series), the differences are striking — all traceable to d-electron involvement in metallic bonding.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`PHYSICAL PROPERTIES · TRANSITION METALS vs Ca`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`Ca`,`Typical first-row TM`],rows:[[`melting point`,`842 °C`,`~1500 °C`],[`density`,`1.55 g cm⁻³`,`~7–9 g cm⁻³`],[`atomic radius`,`197 pm`,`~125 pm`]]},{type:`note`,text:`Transition metals are generally denser, smaller and higher-melting because metallic bonding is stronger.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Physical-property contrast with Ca`},{type:`h`,text:`Why TMs are denser, harder, higher-melting`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In Ca, only 4s electrons contribute to metallic bonding (two electrons per atom in the "sea").`,`In TMs, BOTH 4s and 3d electrons delocalise into the metallic bond. More delocalised electrons → stronger metallic bonds → higher m.p., higher density.`,`Atomic radii are smaller than Ca because nuclear charge is higher while electrons are added to the same inner shell.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why IE and atomic radius are relatively INVARIANT across the series`},{type:`p`,text:`Nuclear charge rises steadily across the period (+1 per element), pulling electrons inward. But the added electrons go into the 3d subshell — INSIDE the 4s. They shield the 4s electrons from the extra nuclear charge. The two effects roughly cancel, so first IE and atomic radius change only gradually from Sc to Zn. Contrast: across Period 3 (s and p block), IE and radius change dramatically.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`TMs: higher m.p./b.p., denser, smaller, less reactive than Ca.`,`Cause: d electrons add to metallic bonding.`,`First IE and atomic radius are relatively constant across first-row TMs.`]}]},{id:`t4`,title:`Variable Oxidation States`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Transition elements form ions in multiple oxidation states because the energies of 4s and 3d electrons are similar — small amounts of energy can strip 3d electrons after the 4s are gone. The number and nature of accessible oxidation states depends on the element.`},{type:`h`,text:`Common oxidation states`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Ti — mainly +4 (TiO₂, TiCl₄)`,`V — +2, +3, +4, +5 (colour-rich chemistry)`,`Cr — +3 most stable; +6 (chromate CrO₄²⁻, dichromate Cr₂O₇²⁻)`,`Mn — +2 (most stable), +4 (MnO₂), +6 (MnO₄²⁻), +7 (MnO₄⁻)`,`Fe — +2, +3 (both important; +3 more stable in air)`,`Co — +2 most common, +3 in some complexes`,`Ni — +2 almost exclusively`,`Cu — +1 (unstable in water — disproportionates), +2 (most stable)`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Oxidation states rise left to right — then drop`,body:`Maximum oxidation state peaks at Mn (+7 = all 7 valence electrons lost). After Mn, the 3d electrons are held too tightly by the increasing nuclear charge to be removed easily — so Fe goes only to +6 (rare), Co to +3, Ni to +2, Cu to +2.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Variable oxidation states = multiple outer electrons at similar energies.`,`Maximum oxidation state peaks at Mn (+7), then declines.`,`+2 is a common oxidation state for most first-row TMs (from removing both 4s).`]}]},{id:`t5`,title:`Complex Ions — Bonding & Shape`,minutes:14,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`A complex ion is a transition-metal ion bonded to ligands. The bonds are dative (coordinate) — both electrons come from the ligand, donated into empty d orbitals on the metal. The number of ligands is the coordination number.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Ligand`,body:`A molecule or ion with a lone pair that can be donated to form a dative bond with a metal centre. Examples: H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, CN⁻, OH⁻.`},{type:`h`,text:`Types of ligand`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Monodentate — donates ONE lone pair. e.g. H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, CN⁻.`,`Bidentate — donates TWO lone pairs at two sites. e.g. ethylenediamine (en), ethanedioate (oxalate).`,`Polydentate (chelating) — donates multiple lone pairs. e.g. EDTA is hexadentate (6 donor sites).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common shapes`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`OCTAHEDRAL · 6 LIGANDS`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:170,primitives:[{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:85,r:16,fill:`primary`,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:89,text:`M`,color:`#000000`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:69,x2:160,y2:32,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:24,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:27,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:101,x2:160,y2:138,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:146,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:149,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:144,y1:85,x2:107,y2:85,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:99,cy:85,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:99,y:88,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:176,y1:85,x2:213,y2:85,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:221,cy:85,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:221,y:88,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:149,y1:96,x2:123,y2:122,color:`text`,width:1.2,dash:!0},{p:`circle`,cx:117,cy:128,r:8,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:117,y:131,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:9},{p:`line`,x1:171,y1:74,x2:197,y2:48,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:203,cy:42,r:8,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:203,y:45,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:9}]},{type:`note`,text:`Bond angle 90° · coordination number 6`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Octahedral — 6 ligands (most common)`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`TETRAHEDRAL · 4 LIGANDS`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:145,primitives:[{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:72,r:16,fill:`primary`,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:76,text:`M`,color:`#000000`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:56,x2:160,y2:24,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:16,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:19,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:146,y1:82,x2:118,y2:102,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:110,cy:108,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:110,y:111,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:174,y1:82,x2:202,y2:102,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`circle`,cx:210,cy:108,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:210,y:111,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:88,x2:160,y2:120,color:`text`,width:1.2,dash:!0},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:128,r:8,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:131,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:9}]},{type:`note`,text:`Bond angle 109.5° · coordination number 4`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Tetrahedral — 4 ligands (especially with large ligands)`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`SQUARE PLANAR · 4 LIGANDS`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:130,primitives:[{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:65,r:16,fill:`primary`,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:69,text:`M`,color:`#000000`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:49,x2:160,y2:22,color:`text`},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:14,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:17,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:81,x2:160,y2:108,color:`text`},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:116,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:119,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:144,y1:65,x2:117,y2:65,color:`text`},{p:`circle`,cx:109,cy:65,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:109,y:68,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`line`,x1:176,y1:65,x2:203,y2:65,color:`text`},{p:`circle`,cx:211,cy:65,r:9,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:211,y:68,text:`L`,color:`text`,size:10}]},{type:`note`,text:`90° bond angles · e.g. [PtCl₄]²⁻ and [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Square planar — 4 ligands (common for d⁸ ions like Ni²⁺, Pt²⁺)`},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Predicting the shape`,body:`Coordination 6 → octahedral. Coordination 4 with small ligands → tetrahedral (e.g. [CoCl₄]²⁻ with Cl⁻). Coordination 4 with d⁸ metal → square planar (e.g. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, [PtCl₄]²⁻). Coordination 2 → linear (e.g. [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Complex = metal + ligands with dative bonds.`,`Coord 6 → octahedral; coord 4 → tetrahedral or square planar; coord 2 → linear.`,`d⁸ metals tend to be square planar.`]}]},{id:`t6`,title:`Isomerism in Complexes`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Complexes with two or more different ligands can exist as different arrangements in space. Three types of isomerism matter at this level.`},{type:`h`,text:`Cis / trans (geometric) isomerism`},{type:`p`,text:`Appears in square-planar complexes with two different kinds of ligand, and in octahedral complexes of the type [ML₄L'₂].`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CIS/TRANS · [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`cis isomer`,color:`primary`,body:[`NH₃ ligands adjacent`,`Cl ligands adjacent`,`cisplatin: anti-cancer activity`]},right:{title:`trans isomer`,color:`accent`,body:[`NH₃ ligands opposite`,`Cl ligands opposite`,`transplatin: inactive`]}},{type:`note`,text:`Square-planar complexes can show geometrical (cis/trans) isomerism.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`cis vs trans [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] — one form is an anti-cancer drug, the other is not`},{type:`h`,text:`Optical isomerism`},{type:`p`,text:`Complexes with three bidentate ligands (e.g. [Ni(en)₃]²⁺) exist as non-superimposable mirror images — just like the classic tetrahedral carbon case in organic chemistry. The two forms rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions.`},{type:`h`,text:`Why it matters`},{type:`ul`,items:[`cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] (cisplatin) binds DNA → used as a chemotherapy drug.`,`trans-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] is biologically inactive — the geometry changes function.`,`Stereochemistry of complexes is essential in modern medicinal chemistry.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`cis/trans: square planar + octahedral complexes with different ligands.`,`Optical: complexes with ≥ 3 bidentate ligands form enantiomer pairs.`,`Geometry changes function — cisplatin is the textbook example.`]}]},{id:`t7`,title:`Ligand Exchange & Stability Constants`,minutes:12,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Ligands in a complex are not permanently attached. They can be swapped for other ligands if the new arrangement is more thermodynamically stable. The equilibrium constant for the exchange is called a stability constant (Kstab).`},{type:`h`,text:`Example — Cu²⁺`},{type:`ul`,items:[`[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (pale blue) + 4NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ (deep blue) + 4H₂O     (NH₃ displaces 4 H₂O)`,`[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CuCl₄]²⁻ (yellow-green) + 6H₂O     (Cl⁻ gives tetrahedral complex, changes colour AND shape)`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Stability constant (Kstab)`,body:`The equilibrium constant for the formation of a complex from the aqua complex. A large Kstab means the product complex is very stable — the new ligand holds on tightly.`},{type:`h`,text:`Typical Kstab values`},{type:`ul`,items:[`[Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺   Kstab ≈ 1.3 × 10¹³  (very stable)`,`[CuCl₄]²⁻                Kstab ≈ 4.2 × 10⁵  (moderately stable)`,`[CuEDTA]²⁻              Kstab ≈ 6.3 × 10¹⁸  (extremely stable — EDTA wraps around in 6 positions)`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Chelate effect`,body:`Polydentate ligands (EDTA, en) form much more stable complexes than the equivalent number of monodentate ligands. Reason: entropy. One EDTA replaces 6 water molecules — six particles become seven (EDTA + 6 free H₂O) → ΔS is large and positive → ΔG more negative.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Ligands exchange if the new complex is more stable (higher Kstab).`,`Polydentate ligands form very stable (chelate) complexes — entropy driver.`,`Ligand exchange can change colour AND shape simultaneously.`]}]},{id:`t8`,title:`Colour of Transition Complexes`,minutes:13,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`When a ligand approaches a transition-metal ion, the five d orbitals that were degenerate (same energy) in the isolated ion are split into groups of slightly different energies. An electron can absorb a photon of visible light to jump from a lower to higher d orbital — the absorbed colour is removed from white light, and we see the complementary colour.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`d-ORBITAL SPLITTING · OCTAHEDRAL FIELD`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:175,primitives:[{p:`line`,x1:40,y1:90,x2:110,y2:90,color:`text`,width:2},{p:`text`,x:75,y:110,text:`5 degenerate d`,color:`dim`,size:9},{p:`line`,x1:170,y1:45,x2:210,y2:45,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:220,y1:45,x2:260,y2:45,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`text`,x:215,y:34,text:`e₉ (dz², dx²−y²)`,color:`primary`,size:9,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:170,y1:130,x2:200,y2:130,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:210,y1:130,x2:240,y2:130,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:250,y1:130,x2:280,y2:130,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`text`,x:225,y:148,text:`t₂g (dxy, dxz, dyz)`,color:`primary`,size:9,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:140,y1:45,x2:140,y2:130,color:`accent`,width:1.2},{p:`text`,x:149,y:90,text:`Δ`,color:`accent`,size:11,bold:!0,anchor:`start`}]},{type:`note`,text:`When Δ matches visible-light photon energy, d–d absorption produces colour.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`d-orbital splitting in an octahedral field`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`COMPLEMENTARY COLOURS`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Absorbed`,`Observed`],rows:[[`red`,`green`],[`orange`,`blue`],[`yellow`,`violet`],[`green`,`red`],[`blue`,`orange`],[`violet`,`yellow`]],rowColors:[`red`,`#f77f00`,`amber`,`green`,`#4361ee`,`#7209b7`]},{type:`note`,text:`A complex appears the complementary colour of the wavelength it absorbs.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Absorbed colour → complementary colour seen`},{type:`h`,text:`What sets the size of the splitting (Δ)?`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Nature of the ligand — strong-field ligands (CN⁻, CO) cause large Δ → absorb high-energy blue/violet → complex looks red/orange. Weak-field ligands (F⁻, H₂O) cause small Δ → absorb red → complex looks blue-green.`,`Oxidation state of metal — higher oxidation state → larger Δ (electrons more tightly held).`,`Geometry — octahedral gives larger Δ than tetrahedral (same ligands).`]},{type:`h`,text:`The spectrochemical series (rough ligand-strength ranking)`},{type:`p`,text:`I⁻ < Br⁻ < Cl⁻ < F⁻ < OH⁻ < H₂O < NH₃ < en < CN⁻ < CO      (weak → strong field)`},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Why colour changes on ligand exchange`,body:`Different ligands give different Δ values → different photons absorbed → different complementary colour seen. That's why [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is pale blue, [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ is deep blue, and [CuCl₄]²⁻ is yellow-green.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Why Sc³⁺, Zn²⁺, Cu⁺ are colourless`,body:`No partly-filled d subshell means no d-d transition is possible. Sc³⁺ is d⁰, Zn²⁺ and Cu⁺ are d¹⁰. All three are colourless in solution.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Colour comes from d-d electron transitions.`,`Δ depends on ligand strength, metal oxidation state, geometry.`,`Complementary-colour rule: seen ≈ complement of absorbed.`,`d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions are colourless.`]}]},{id:`t9`,title:`Catalysis & Magnetism`,minutes:12,blocks:[{type:`h`,text:`Catalytic activity`},{type:`p`,text:`Transition elements are excellent catalysts, in elemental form (heterogeneous) and as ions (homogeneous). Two reasons: variable oxidation states allow the catalyst to be both oxidised and reduced during a reaction cycle; the d orbitals can accept and donate electrons flexibly.`},{type:`h`,text:`Important industrial examples`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Fe — Haber process (N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃). Heterogeneous, reactants adsorb on iron surface.`,`V₂O₅ — Contact process (2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃). V₂O₅ oxidises SO₂ to SO₃, becomes V₂O₄, re-oxidised by O₂.`,`Ni — Catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes to alkanes (and oils to margarine).`,`Pt / Rh / Pd — three-way catalytic converter in cars; also hydrogenations and Haber-process alternatives.`,`MnO₂ — decomposition of H₂O₂ in the lab; Mn²⁺ catalyses redox of S₂O₈²⁻ + I⁻.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Homogeneous catalysis — change in oxidation state`,body:`In Mn²⁺ catalysis of S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂: Mn²⁺ is oxidised to Mn³⁺ by S₂O₈²⁻, then Mn³⁺ reduces I⁻ to I₂, regenerating Mn²⁺. The catalyst shuttles between two oxidation states.`},{type:`h`,text:`Magnetic properties`},{type:`p`,text:`The magnetic behaviour of a complex depends on the number of unpaired electrons in its d subshell.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Diamagnetic — all d electrons paired. Weakly repelled by a magnetic field.`,`Paramagnetic — some d electrons unpaired. Weakly attracted to a magnetic field. Magnetic moment increases with more unpaired electrons.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Predicting unpaired electrons`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Zn²⁺ (d¹⁰) — 0 unpaired. Diamagnetic.`,`Cu²⁺ (d⁹) — 1 unpaired. Paramagnetic.`,`Fe²⁺ (d⁶) — up to 4 unpaired (high-spin) or 0 (low-spin). Depends on ligand.`,`Fe³⁺ (d⁵) — up to 5 unpaired. Strongly paramagnetic.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`High-spin vs low-spin`,body:`Weak ligands (H₂O, F⁻) give small Δ — electrons spread out to follow Hund's rule → high-spin → more unpaired. Strong ligands (CN⁻, CO) give large Δ — pairing is cheaper than jumping to the upper set → low-spin → fewer unpaired.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Catalysis: variable oxidation states + flexible d-orbital involvement.`,`Paramagnetism scales with unpaired d electrons.`,`Strong ligands → low-spin; weak ligands → high-spin.`]}]},{id:`t10`,title:`Vanadium — Colourful Redox`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Vanadium is the poster child for variable oxidation states — four common states, each a different colour. Reduction of VO₂⁺ (+5) with zinc in acid can be followed by colour through yellow → blue → green → violet.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`VANADIUM · OXIDATION STATES`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Ion`,`Ox. state`,`Colour`],rows:[[`VO₂⁺`,`+5`,`yellow`],[`VO²⁺`,`+4`,`blue`],[`V³⁺`,`+3`,`green`],[`V²⁺`,`+2`,`violet`]],rowColors:[`amber`,`#4361ee`,`green`,`#7209b7`]},{type:`note`,text:`Zn/H⁺ reduces V stepwise: +5 → +4 → +3 → +2.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`V oxidation states — one of the most-tested patterns`},{type:`h`,text:`Occurrence and uses`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Occurs as vanadinite Pb₅(VO₄)₃Cl and carnotite K₂(UO₂)₂(VO₄)₂.`,`Ferrovanadium alloy — high-strength steels (tools, engine parts).`,`V₂O₅ — industrial catalyst in the Contact process for sulphuric acid (explains why vanadium is critical despite being rare).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Observing the redox`,body:`Add Zn metal to acidified VO₂⁺(aq). Yellow slowly turns blue (VO²⁺), then green (V³⁺), then pale violet (V²⁺). Each step is a one-electron reduction. Reverse by oxidising with KMnO₄ or air.`}]},{id:`t11`,title:`Chromium — +3 and +6`,minutes:12,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Chromium's important oxidation states at A-level are +3 (stable, green or violet) and +6 (yellow chromate or orange dichromate — strong oxidising agents).`},{type:`h`,text:`Chromate ⇌ dichromate equilibrium`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CHROMATE ⇌ DICHROMATE`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`CrO₄²⁻`,color:`amber`,body:[`yellow`,`Cr = +6`,`favoured by alkaline conditions`]},right:{title:`Cr₂O₇²⁻`,color:`#f77f00`,body:[`orange`,`Cr = +6`,`favoured by acidic conditions`]}},{type:`equation`,text:`2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O`},{type:`note`,text:`Add H⁺: yellow → orange. Add OH⁻: orange → yellow.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Interconversion controlled by pH`},{type:`ul`,items:[`2 CrO₄²⁻(aq) + 2 H⁺(aq) ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)`,`Add acid → shift right → orange (dichromate).`,`Add alkali → shift left → yellow (chromate).`,`Both species are Cr(VI). No change in oxidation state — only in structure.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Dichromate as oxidising agent`},{type:`p`,text:`In acidic solution, Cr₂O₇²⁻ is reduced from orange (+6) to green Cr³⁺ (+3). Half-equation: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. Useful for oxidising:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Primary alcohols → aldehydes → carboxylic acids.`,`Secondary alcohols → ketones.`,`SO₂ → SO₄²⁻. Can be used as a test for SO₂: orange-to-green colour change.`,`Alcohol in breathalysers (traditional yellow-to-green test).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Occurrence and uses`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Ore: chromite, FeCr₂O₄.`,`Used in stainless steel (Cr + Ni + Fe) — Cr forms a protective Cr₂O₃ layer.`,`Chrome plating — decorative and corrosion-resistant coating.`,`Cr₂O₃ as a green pigment; Cr₂O₇²⁻ historically as a cleaning oxidant (largely phased out due to Cr(VI) toxicity).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Cr(VI) is toxic`,body:`Chromates and dichromates are carcinogenic. Modern chemistry tries to avoid Cr(VI) oxidants, preferring greener alternatives (Jones oxidation replaced by Dess-Martin, etc.).`}]},{id:`t12`,title:`Manganese — The Redox Workhorse`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Manganese shows +2, +4, +6, +7. The most useful in the lab is MnO₄⁻ (permanganate), a dark purple strong oxidising agent.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`MANGANESE · KEY OXIDATION STATES`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Species`,`Ox. state`,`Colour`,`Key point`],rows:[[`MnO₄⁻`,`+7`,`purple`,`strong oxidiser`],[`MnO₄²⁻`,`+6`,`green`,`disproportionates`],[`MnO₂`,`+4`,`black`,`catalyst`],[`Mn²⁺`,`+2`,`pale pink`,`stable in acid`]],rowColors:[`#7209b7`,`green`,`#2d3748`,`#ffcccc`]},{type:`equation`,text:`3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O`}]},caption:`Key Mn oxidation states`},{type:`h`,text:`Permanganate as oxidising agent (acidic conditions)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Half-equation: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O  (E° = +1.51 V)`,`Colour change: intense purple → almost colourless (Mn²⁺ pale pink).`,`Self-indicating in titrations — the first persistent pink marks the endpoint.`,`Used in titrations of: Fe²⁺ (→ Fe³⁺), ethanedioic acid (→ CO₂), H₂O₂ (→ O₂).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Disproportionation of MnO₄²⁻`},{type:`p`,text:`Manganate(VI) is unstable in acidic solution — it disproportionates into Mn(VII) and Mn(IV):`},{type:`ul`,items:[`3 MnO₄²⁻ + 4 H⁺ → 2 MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2 H₂O`,`Same element (Mn) is both oxidised (+6 → +7) and reduced (+6 → +4).`,`MnO₂ is the brown-black solid (a classic disproportionation product).`]},{type:`h`,text:`MnO₂ as catalyst`},{type:`p`,text:`MnO₂ catalyses the decomposition of H₂O₂: 2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂. A safe classroom demonstration — add a pinch of MnO₂ to a bottle of dilute H₂O₂ and O₂ evolves vigorously.`}]},{id:`t13`,title:`Iron — Extraction, Biology, Rusting`,minutes:14,blocks:[{type:`h`,text:`Occurrence and extraction`},{type:`p`,text:`Iron ores include hematite (Fe₂O₃) and magnetite (Fe₃O₄). Extraction in a blast furnace uses coke (C), limestone (CaCO₃) and hot air:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`C + O₂ → CO₂     (coke burns)`,`CO₂ + C → 2CO    (regeneration of reducing agent)`,`Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂   (reduction of ore)`,`CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂   (decomposition of flux)`,`CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃   (slag formation — removes impurities)`]},{type:`h`,text:`Fe²⁺ vs Fe³⁺ — tests and stability`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Fe²⁺ + K₃[Fe(CN)₆] → deep blue (Turnbull's blue).`,`Fe³⁺ + K₄[Fe(CN)₆] → deep blue (Prussian blue).`,`Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ → blood-red [Fe(SCN)(H₂O)₅]²⁺.`,`Fe²⁺ + NaOH(aq) → dirty-green Fe(OH)₂ ppt, darkens on standing (air-oxidation).`,`Fe³⁺ + NaOH(aq) → red-brown Fe(OH)₃ ppt.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Effect of pH and ligand on stability`,body:`Fe³⁺ is more stable than Fe²⁺ in air (oxidised by O₂). BUT the standard electrode potential depends on the ligands. With CN⁻ ligand (strong field), [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻/[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ has E° = +0.36 V — much less than Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ (+0.77 V) — meaning CN⁻ stabilises Fe²⁺ more than Fe³⁺. Ligand choice changes which oxidation state is favoured.`},{type:`h`,text:`Biological importance`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`IRON IN BIOLOGY`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`HAEMOGLOBIN`,body:[`Fe²⁺ sits in a porphyrin (haem) ring`,`binds O₂ reversibly`,`carries O₂ from lungs to tissues`],color:`red`},{type:`panel`,title:`CARBON MONOXIDE`,body:[`CO binds haem much more strongly than O₂ (~250×)`,`blocks oxygen transport → toxic effect`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Iron also occurs in cytochromes used in electron transport.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Iron in haemoglobin`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Haemoglobin — Fe²⁺ at the centre of a porphyrin ring, reversibly binding O₂ for transport.`,`Cytochromes — Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ cycling in electron-transport chains (energy production).`,`Myoglobin — O₂ storage in muscle.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Rusting`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`RUSTING · BOTH O₂ AND H₂O REQUIRED`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`ANODE · iron oxidation`,body:[`Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`CATHODE · oxygen reduction`,body:[`O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`RUST FORMATION`,body:[`Fe²⁺ is further oxidised to Fe³⁺`,`hydrated iron(III) oxide forms: Fe₂O₃·xH₂O`],color:`red`},{type:`note`,text:`Rusting is an electrochemical corrosion process.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Electrochemical rusting needs O₂ AND water`},{type:`h`,text:`Rust prevention`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Barrier methods — paint, grease, plastic coating, plating (Zn, Cr, Ni, Sn).`,`Galvanising — zinc coating. Zn is more reactive; even if scratched, Zn corrodes preferentially (sacrificial protection).`,`Cathodic protection — attach a block of Mg or Zn (sacrificial anode) to the iron; it oxidises in place of the iron. Used on ships, pipelines.`,`Alloying — stainless steel (Fe + Cr + Ni) forms passive Cr₂O₃ layer.`]}]},{id:`t14`,title:`Co · Ni · Cu · Platinum Group Metals`,minutes:14,blocks:[{type:`h`,text:`Cobalt — +2 and +3`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`COBALT · LIGAND / TEMPERATURE EFFECTS`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺`,color:`#ffcccc`,body:[`pink`,`octahedral`,`favoured by water / lower Cl⁻`]},right:{title:`[CoCl₄]²⁻`,color:`#4361ee`,body:[`blue`,`tetrahedral`,`favoured by conc. HCl / heat`]}},{type:`equation`,text:`[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CoCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O`},{type:`note`,text:`Cobalt(II) chloride paper: blue (anhydrous) → pink (hydrated).`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Pink → blue cobalt on ligand/temperature change`},{type:`ul`,items:[`[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ — pink, octahedral. Stable at room temperature in water.`,`[CoCl₄]²⁻ — blue, tetrahedral. Formed on adding conc. HCl and/or warming.`,`[Co(NH₃)₆]²⁺ — straw-coloured; oxidises in air to yellow [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ — NH₃ stabilises the +3 state.`,`Uses: alloys (alnico magnets), Vitamin B₁₂ (the only biologically essential Co compound), anhydrous CoCl₂ paper as a simple humidity/water indicator (blue → pink).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Nickel`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Mainly +2 oxidation state; green [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺.`,`Extraction from pentlandite (Ni,Fe)₉S₈ — roasting, reduction, Mond process (volatilisation as Ni(CO)₄ then thermal decomposition).`,`Uses: stainless steel, alnico, coinage, Raney nickel and Ni/Al₂O₃ as hydrogenation catalysts (margarine, catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Copper — +1 and +2`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`COPPER(I) DISPROPORTIONATION`,blocks:[{type:`equation`,text:`2Cu⁺(aq) → Cu(s) + Cu²⁺(aq)`,big:!0},{type:`compare`,left:{title:`Reduction`,color:`green`,body:[`Cu⁺ + e⁻ → Cu`,`oxidation state +1 → 0`]},right:{title:`Oxidation`,color:`accent`,body:[`Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + e⁻`,`oxidation state +1 → +2`]}},{type:`note`,text:`Cu⁺ is unstable in aqueous solution but can be stabilised by suitable ligands / solids such as CuI and CuCN.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Cu⁺ disproportionation`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cu⁺ (d¹⁰) is colourless and unstable in water — disproportionates to Cu and Cu²⁺.`,`Cu⁺ is stabilised with certain ligands — CuI and [Cu(CN)₂]⁻ exist because of low solubility / strong ligand binding.`,`Cu²⁺ (d⁹) — the common state. Pale blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺; deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺; yellow-green [CuCl₄]²⁻.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Cu²⁺ + I⁻ — unusual redox`},{type:`p`,text:`At first glance, Cu²⁺ should NOT oxidise I⁻ (E°(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = +0.15 V, E°(I₂/I⁻) = +0.54 V). But it does: 2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → 2CuI(s) + I₂. Why? Because CuI is very insoluble — formation of solid CuI drives the equilibrium forward (Le Chatelier). Ligand/lattice effects on E° pay for the reaction.`},{type:`h`,text:`Copper extraction & uses`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Main ores: chalcopyrite (CuFeS₂), malachite (Cu₂CO₃(OH)₂).`,`Purification: electrolytic refining — impure Cu anode, pure Cu cathode, CuSO₄ electrolyte. Valuable impurities (Ag, Au) collect as anode slime.`,`Uses: electrical wiring (high conductivity), brass (Cu + Zn), bronze (Cu + Sn), Fehling's/Benedict's reagent for aldehydes (blue Cu²⁺ → brick-red Cu₂O).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Platinum Group Metals (PGMs)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Six elements: ruthenium, rhodium, palladium, osmium, iridium, platinum. Occur together in nature.`,`Main source: South African / Zimbabwean Great Dyke platinum-bearing ores; by-product of Ni mining.`,`Uses: catalytic converters (Pt/Pd/Rh), jewellery, electronics, industrial catalysts (Pt in Contact process before V₂O₅), medical (cisplatin for cancer chemotherapy).`,`High economic importance: PGMs are a significant export from Zimbabwe and South Africa.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Cobalt — pink H₂O, blue Cl⁻, NH₃ stabilises +3.`,`Nickel — almost always +2, major hydrogenation catalyst.`,`Copper — Cu⁺ disproportionates in water except when stabilised by special ligands.`,`PGMs — Zimbabwe's Great Dyke is a key world source.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`What defines a transition element?`,opts:[`Any d-block element`,`A d-block element that forms at least one ion with a partially-filled d subshell`,`Any element with a d-orbital`,`Any element heavier than calcium`],ans:1,hint:`Sc and Zn are d-block but fail the test.`},{q:`Why is Zn NOT a transition element?`,opts:[`It's in the s-block`,`Zn²⁺ has a full d¹⁰ configuration`,`It's too soft`,`It forms no compounds`],ans:1,hint:`d¹⁰ = full, not partial.`},{q:`Electron configuration of Cr (Z=24) is:`,opts:[`[Ar] 3d⁴ 4s²`,`[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹`,`[Ar] 3d⁶`,`[Ar] 4s² 4p⁴`],ans:1,hint:`Half-filled d stability.`},{q:`When forming Fe³⁺, which electrons leave first?`,opts:[`Three 3d electrons`,`Both 4s first, then one 3d`,`Three 3p electrons`,`One 2p, two 3d`],ans:1,hint:`4s empties before 3d.`},{q:`Fe³⁺ has electronic configuration:`,opts:[`[Ar] 3d³`,`[Ar] 3d⁵`,`[Ar] 3d⁶`,`[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹`],ans:1,hint:`Fe is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². Remove 3 electrons (2 × 4s + 1 × 3d).`},{q:`Why do TMs have higher melting points than Ca?`,opts:[`More valence electrons`,`Both 4s AND 3d electrons contribute to metallic bonding`,`Smaller radius`,`Lower atomic mass`],ans:1,hint:`More delocalised electrons → stronger bonds.`},{q:`Across the first-row TM series, first IE:`,opts:[`Rises sharply`,`Remains relatively constant`,`Falls sharply`,`Falls then rises`],ans:1,hint:`Added 3d electrons shield added nuclear charge.`},{q:`The maximum oxidation state in the first row peaks at:`,opts:[`Sc`,`Cr`,`Mn (+7)`,`Fe`],ans:2,hint:`Mn has 7 removable outer electrons.`},{q:`In a complex ion, the metal-ligand bond is:`,opts:[`Ionic`,`Covalent σ bond with equal donation`,`Dative (coordinate) — both electrons from ligand`,`Hydrogen bond`],ans:2,hint:`Ligand donates a lone pair.`},{q:`Which shape is most common for coordination number 6?`,opts:[`Tetrahedral`,`Square planar`,`Octahedral`,`Trigonal bipyramidal`],ans:2,hint:`Six ligands at 90°.`},{q:`[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is:`,opts:[`Tetrahedral`,`Square planar`,`Octahedral`,`Linear`],ans:1,hint:`d⁸ metal + strong-field ligand → square planar.`},{q:`Which ligand is bidentate?`,opts:[`H₂O`,`NH₃`,`Ethylenediamine (en)`,`Cl⁻`],ans:2,hint:`Bidentate = two donor atoms.`},{q:`EDTA is:`,opts:[`Monodentate`,`Bidentate`,`Tridentate`,`Hexadentate`],ans:3,hint:`Six donor atoms wrap around the metal.`},{q:`Why are chelate complexes especially stable?`,opts:[`Stronger bonds`,`Entropy increase — one polydentate ligand displaces many water molecules`,`Lighter`,`Less colourful`],ans:1,hint:`ΔS ≫ 0 for the substitution.`},{q:`Colour in TM complexes arises from:`,opts:[`f-f transitions`,`Electron jumps between split d orbitals (d-d)`,`Vibrations of the ligand`,`Emission from heated metal`],ans:1,hint:`Δ corresponds to a visible photon.`},{q:`Why is Zn²⁺ colourless?`,opts:[`d⁰ configuration`,`d¹⁰ — no d-d transition possible`,`No d orbitals`,`Light can't reach it`],ans:1,hint:`Fully filled = no empty d to jump to.`},{q:`A complex absorbs green light. What colour do we see?`,opts:[`Blue`,`Red`,`Yellow`,`Green`],ans:1,hint:`We see the complementary colour.`},{q:`Which ligand causes the LARGEST splitting Δ?`,opts:[`I⁻`,`H₂O`,`NH₃`,`CN⁻`],ans:3,hint:`Spectrochemical series — CN⁻ is very strong field.`},{q:`Fe²⁺ (d⁶) with weak-field ligands is high-spin. Number of unpaired electrons:`,opts:[`0`,`2`,`4`,`6`],ans:2,hint:`Hund fills 5 then pairs one. 4 unpaired.`},{q:`Fe²⁺ (d⁶) with strong-field ligands (e.g. CN⁻) is low-spin. Unpaired e⁻:`,opts:[`0`,`2`,`4`,`6`],ans:0,hint:`All six pair in lower t₂g set.`},{q:`Which colour combination matches vanadium oxidation states correctly?`,opts:[`+5 violet, +2 yellow`,`+5 yellow, +4 blue, +3 green, +2 violet`,`All purple`,`+2 red, +5 orange`],ans:1,hint:`Yellow → blue → green → violet as you reduce.`},{q:`Adding acid to yellow chromate gives:`,opts:[`Green Cr³⁺`,`Orange dichromate`,`Black MnO₂`,`Colourless`],ans:1,hint:`2 CrO₄²⁻ + 2 H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O.`},{q:`What is reduced when K₂Cr₂O₇/H⁺ oxidises ethanol?`,opts:[`Cr³⁺ → Cr²⁺`,`Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange, +6) → Cr³⁺ (green, +3)`,`Cr → Cr²⁺`,`Cr₂O₇²⁻ → Cr₂O₃`],ans:1,hint:`+6 to +3, orange to green.`},{q:`The disproportionation of MnO₄²⁻ in acid produces:`,opts:[`MnO₄⁻ only`,`Mn²⁺ only`,`MnO₄⁻ and MnO₂`,`Mn₂O₃`],ans:2,hint:`Same element both oxidised (+6→+7) and reduced (+6→+4).`},{q:`In the Fe³⁺/SCN⁻ test, the observation is:`,opts:[`White ppt`,`Blood-red solution`,`Blue solution`,`Orange ppt`],ans:1,hint:`[Fe(SCN)(H₂O)₅]²⁺.`},{q:`Why is CO toxic?`,opts:[`It explodes`,`It binds haemoglobin ~250× more strongly than O₂, blocking O₂ transport`,`It displaces Fe from haemoglobin`,`It acidifies blood`],ans:1,hint:`Same Fe binding site, much tighter grip.`},{q:`Rusting requires:`,opts:[`Iron only`,`Iron + O₂ only`,`Iron + H₂O only`,`Iron + O₂ + H₂O`],ans:3,hint:`Both are needed — classic experiment.`},{q:`In galvanising, zinc:`,opts:[`Acts as a physical barrier only`,`Is a sacrificial anode — oxidises preferentially`,`Catalyses Fe`,`Hardens the Fe`],ans:1,hint:`Zn is more reactive than Fe → oxidises first.`},{q:`[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is pink. Adding conc. HCl gives:`,opts:[`Green octahedral`,`Blue tetrahedral [CoCl₄]²⁻`,`Red octahedral`,`Colourless solution`],ans:1,hint:`Ligand exchange + geometry change.`},{q:`Anhydrous CoCl₂ is used to test for:`,opts:[`O₂`,`Water (blue → pink)`,`NH₃`,`H₂`],ans:1,hint:`Colour change on hydration.`},{q:`Ni is particularly useful as:`,opts:[`A catalyst for hydrogenation`,`An explosive`,`A dye`,`An acid`],ans:0,hint:`Ni/Al₂O₃, Raney Ni — hydrogenation.`},{q:`Cu⁺ is unstable in water because:`,opts:[`It's too small`,`It disproportionates: 2Cu⁺ → Cu + Cu²⁺`,`It reacts with water to give H₂`,`It evaporates`],ans:1,hint:`Same element both oxidised and reduced.`},{q:`Adding NH₃(aq) to [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ in excess gives:`,opts:[`Brown precipitate`,`Deep blue solution [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺`,`Green solution`,`No change`],ans:1,hint:`Ligand exchange; 4 H₂O replaced by 4 NH₃.`},{q:`The Cu²⁺ + I⁻ reaction works because:`,opts:[`CuI is soluble`,`CuI is very insoluble, which drives the equilibrium right`,`I⁻ is a strong ligand`,`Cu²⁺ is unstable`],ans:1,hint:`Lattice energy pays for the unfavourable redox.`},{q:`The purification of Cu uses:`,opts:[`Blast furnace`,`Electrolytic refining with impure anode + pure cathode`,`Fractional distillation`,`Cracking`],ans:1,hint:`CuSO₄ electrolyte, impurities in anode slime.`},{q:`Platinum group metals are important for:`,opts:[`Catalytic converters in car exhausts`,`Iron extraction`,`Water purification`,`Margarine production`],ans:0,hint:`Pt/Pd/Rh three-way catalyst.`},{q:`Which Zimbabwean geological feature is a major source of PGMs?`,opts:[`Mount Nyangani`,`Kariba Dam`,`Great Dyke`,`Victoria Falls`],ans:2,hint:`Runs north-south through Zimbabwe.`}],multi:[{q:`Which statements about transition-metal complexes are TRUE?`,opts:[`1. Colour arises from d-d electron transitions`,`2. Strong-field ligands cause smaller splitting Δ`,`3. Ligand-metal bonds are dative (coordinate)`,`4. d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions are colourless`],ans:[0,2,3],hint:`Strong-field ligands give LARGER Δ, not smaller.`},{q:`Regarding variable oxidation states of transition metals:`,opts:[`1. They arise because 3d and 4s are close in energy`,`2. Maximum oxidation state occurs at Mn (+7) in the first row`,`3. Sc has the widest range of common oxidation states`,`4. Fe²⁺ is more stable than Fe³⁺ in aerated water`],ans:[0,1],hint:`Sc is almost always +3 only. Fe³⁺ is favoured in air.`},{q:`Which are TRUE about the Cu²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O reaction?`,opts:[`1. Ligand exchange from water to chloride`,`2. Geometry changes from octahedral to tetrahedral`,`3. Oxidation state of Cu changes`,`4. Colour changes from pale blue to yellow-green`],ans:[0,1,3],hint:`Cu stays +2 throughout.`},{q:`Which apply to rusting?`,opts:[`1. Requires both O₂ and H₂O`,`2. Involves electrochemical (cathode/anode) behaviour on the iron surface`,`3. Is prevented effectively by painting only`,`4. Can be prevented by sacrificial protection with a more reactive metal`],ans:[0,1,3],hint:`Paint alone fails if scratched — needs full coverage or sacrificial metal.`}],structured:[{title:`Defining transition elements`,marks:8,q:`Using the first-row d-block as reference:`,parts:[{q:`(a) Define a transition element.`,marks:2,ans:`A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a partially-filled d subshell.`},{q:`(b) Explain why Zn is NOT considered a transition element.`,marks:2,ans:`Zn forms only Zn²⁺, which has a d¹⁰ (fully-filled) configuration. There is no ion of zinc with a partially-filled d subshell, so Zn does not meet the definition of a transition element.`},{q:`(c) Give the electron configuration of Cr and of Cu, and explain why they are anomalous.`,marks:3,ans:`Cr: [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ (not 3d⁴ 4s²). Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (not 3d⁹ 4s²). Both anomalies arise because half-filled (d⁵) and fully-filled (d¹⁰) d subshells have extra stability, making the configuration with one 4s electron and an optimally-filled 3d energetically preferred.`},{q:`(d) Write the configuration of Fe³⁺ and state why 4s electrons are removed before 3d.`,marks:1,ans:`Fe³⁺: [Ar] 3d⁵. Although 4s fills before 3d in the neutral atom (4s slightly lower), once 3d has electrons, 3d is lower in energy than 4s, so 4s is removed first on ionisation.`}]},{title:`Complex ions — shape, colour, ligand exchange`,marks:10,q:`Cu²⁺ exists as [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ in water (pale blue). It reacts with excess NH₃ and with concentrated HCl.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Give the structures (formulas) of the complexes formed with (i) excess NH₃, (ii) conc. HCl.`,marks:2,ans:`(i) [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ (deep blue, octahedral — 4 NH₃ replace 4 equatorial H₂O, 2 axial H₂O remain). (ii) [CuCl₄]²⁻ (yellow-green, tetrahedral).`},{q:`(b) Describe and explain the colour change for each reaction.`,marks:3,ans:`(i) pale blue → deep blue: NH₃ is a stronger-field ligand than H₂O, increasing Δ, which shifts the absorbed wavelength to a slightly different region and gives a deeper blue. (ii) pale blue → yellow-green: Cl⁻ is a weaker-field ligand than H₂O AND the geometry changes from octahedral to tetrahedral, both reducing Δ substantially; the absorbed wavelength shifts to red, and the complex looks yellow-green.`},{q:`(c) Explain, in terms of coordination number and ligand size, why Cu²⁺ forms a 6-coordinate complex with H₂O but only a 4-coordinate complex with Cl⁻.`,marks:3,ans:`Water molecules are small and can fit six around the Cu²⁺ without steric clash. Chloride ions are much larger, so only four can fit around Cu²⁺. The drop in coordination number from 6 to 4 changes the shape from octahedral to tetrahedral.`},{q:`(d) The stability constant of [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ is ~10¹³, while [CuEDTA]²⁻ is ~10¹⁸. Explain briefly why EDTA gives a more stable complex.`,marks:2,ans:`EDTA is a hexadentate ligand — one EDTA molecule replaces all six water molecules around Cu²⁺. This produces six free water molecules from one EDTA, a large entropy gain (ΔS ≫ 0). In contrast, NH₃ replaces only 4 waters, giving a smaller entropy gain. Chelate complexes are always more stable for entropic reasons.`}]},{title:`Chromium and manganese redox`,marks:9,q:`Chromium and manganese both show several oxidation states.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the equilibrium between chromate and dichromate ions, and state the effect of adding acid.`,marks:2,ans:`2 CrO₄²⁻(aq) + 2 H⁺(aq) ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l). Adding acid shifts the equilibrium to the right (Le Chatelier); the yellow chromate colour changes to orange dichromate. Adding alkali reverses this.`},{q:`(b) Using oxidation-state reasoning, explain what happens when acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ reacts with ethanol.`,marks:3,ans:`Ethanol is oxidised to ethanal (and then ethanoic acid if excess dichromate). The dichromate ion is reduced from Cr(VI) in orange Cr₂O₇²⁻ to Cr(III) in green Cr³⁺ solution. Half-equation: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. The orange-to-green colour change is the classic observation.`},{q:`(c) Write the equation for the disproportionation of MnO₄²⁻ in acidic solution, identifying the species oxidised and reduced.`,marks:2,ans:`3 MnO₄²⁻ + 4 H⁺ → 2 MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2 H₂O. Same element (Mn) is both oxidised (+6 → +7 in MnO₄⁻) and reduced (+6 → +4 in MnO₂).`},{q:`(d) State one use of MnO₂ as a catalyst.`,marks:2,ans:`MnO₂ catalyses the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide: 2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂. Classic lab demonstration for preparing oxygen.`}]},{title:`Iron chemistry and extraction`,marks:10,q:`Iron is extracted in a blast furnace from iron ore, coke, limestone, and hot air. The product is used to make steel and is also of great biological importance.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write balanced equations for the three reactions that reduce Fe₂O₃ to Fe.`,marks:3,ans:`C + O₂ → CO₂ (coke burns). CO₂ + C → 2CO (regenerates CO as reducing agent). Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂ (reduction of ore to molten iron).`},{q:`(b) State the role of limestone and give the equation for slag formation.`,marks:2,ans:`Limestone decomposes in the heat: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. The CaO reacts with acidic silica impurities to form molten slag: CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃. The slag floats on the molten iron and is tapped off separately.`},{q:`(c) Describe a chemical test to distinguish Fe²⁺(aq) from Fe³⁺(aq) using potassium thiocyanate.`,marks:2,ans:`Add aqueous KSCN. Fe³⁺ gives an intense blood-red solution due to formation of [Fe(SCN)(H₂O)₅]²⁺. Fe²⁺ gives no colour change (pale green or near-colourless solution remains).`},{q:`(d) Explain the biochemical importance of iron, naming TWO iron-containing biomolecules.`,marks:3,ans:`Haemoglobin: Fe²⁺ at the centre of a porphyrin ring reversibly binds O₂, enabling oxygen transport in the blood. Cytochromes: contain Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ ions that cycle between the two oxidation states to carry electrons during respiration (electron transport chain, ATP production).`}]},{title:`Catalysis and magnetism`,marks:8,q:`Transition elements are the most important group in catalysis and also show characteristic magnetic behaviour.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain why transition metals make good catalysts, mentioning TWO reasons.`,marks:3,ans:`Firstly, they have variable oxidation states — the catalyst can cycle between two states during the reaction, transferring electrons at each stage. Secondly, their partially-filled d orbitals can accept electrons from reactant molecules and donate electrons back, providing alternative reaction pathways with lower activation energies. Also, on solid TMs, reactants adsorb on the surface, weakening their bonds.`},{q:`(b) Give TWO industrial catalytic processes, naming the transition-metal catalyst and the key reaction.`,marks:2,ans:`Fe in the Haber process (N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃). V₂O₅ in the Contact process (2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃). Also acceptable: Ni in catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes. Pt/Rh in catalytic converters.`},{q:`(c) Predict the number of unpaired electrons in high-spin Fe³⁺ and in low-spin Fe²⁺.`,marks:2,ans:`High-spin Fe³⁺ (d⁵): 5 unpaired electrons (all t₂g and eg orbitals singly occupied). Low-spin Fe²⁺ (d⁶): 0 unpaired (all 6 electrons paired in the lower t₂g set). These give very different magnetic moments.`},{q:`(d) Describe how you would tell, from a magnetic measurement, whether a Fe²⁺ complex was high-spin or low-spin.`,marks:1,ans:`Measure the complex's magnetic moment. A high-spin Fe²⁺ complex has 4 unpaired electrons and is strongly paramagnetic. A low-spin Fe²⁺ complex has 0 unpaired electrons and is diamagnetic (weakly repelled by a magnet).`}]}],worked:[{title:`Electron configuration of a transition-metal ion`,diff:`easy`,q:`Write the electron configuration of Mn²⁺.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Mn has Z = 25. Neutral atom: [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s².`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`To form Mn²⁺ remove 2 electrons. 4s leaves first.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Both 4s electrons are removed, 3d untouched.`}],ans:`Mn²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁵`},{title:`Predict complex shape`,diff:`easy`,q:`Predict the shape of [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and [CoCl₄]²⁻.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Count ligands. [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has 6 NH₃ ligands; [CoCl₄]²⁻ has 4 Cl⁻ ligands.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Coordination 6 → octahedral (nearly always at A-level).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Coordination 4 with small halide ligand → tetrahedral (Co²⁺ is NOT d⁸ so not square planar).`}],ans:`[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is octahedral; [CoCl₄]²⁻ is tetrahedral.`},{title:`Balance redox in acidic solution`,diff:`medium`,q:`Balance the equation for MnO₄⁻ oxidising Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ in acidic solution.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Write the half-equations. Reduction: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Oxidation: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Balance electrons — multiply Fe²⁺ half-equation by 5 to match MnO₄⁻'s 5 e⁻.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Add half-equations. Electrons cancel.`}],ans:`MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺`},{title:`Colour prediction`,diff:`medium`,q:`A cobalt complex absorbs red light. Predict its observed colour and state what ligand type is likely present.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Complementary colour rule: absorbs red → we see the complementary colour, which is green.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Red light = low energy. Low energy absorbed means small Δ splitting.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Small Δ is caused by weak-field ligands (low on spectrochemical series) OR tetrahedral geometry OR low metal oxidation state.`}],ans:`Complex appears green. Ligands are weak-field (e.g. H₂O, F⁻, or halides), or geometry is tetrahedral, giving small Δ.`},{title:`Ligand exchange feasibility`,diff:`medium`,q:`The stability constant of [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ is ~10¹⁸; of [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ is ~10⁹. If ethylenediamine (en) is added to [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺, what happens?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Compare stability constants. Kstab([Ni(en)₃]²⁺) ≫ Kstab([Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`The higher Kstab means the en complex is much more thermodynamically stable.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Equilibrium favours the en complex — ligand exchange proceeds. [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ + 3en ⇌ [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ + 6NH₃.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Why is en so much better? It is bidentate — three en molecules = six donor atoms. But displacing six NH₃ with three en gives a net increase in free particles (3 → 6 NH₃ + 3 en becomes → 3 en-complex units + 6 NH₃). Entropy rises → ΔG more negative.`}],ans:`[Ni(en)₃]²⁺ forms, displacing NH₃. The chelate (entropy) effect drives the exchange.`},{title:`Predict colour change after ligand exchange`,diff:`hard`,q:`A pale-blue solution of [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is observed to absorb red light. When excess concentrated ammonia is added, the solution turns deep blue. Use crystal-field reasoning to explain the change.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Apply complementary-colour rule. The starting solution absorbs RED, so it transmits BLUE-GREEN — matches the observed pale blue.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Identify the splitting Δ. Energy of red light is small → Δ is small. This is consistent with H₂O being a moderately weak-field ligand on the spectrochemical series.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Adding excess NH₃ drives ligand exchange. NH₃ replaces 4 of the 6 H₂O ligands: [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`NH₃ sits HIGHER on the spectrochemical series than H₂O — it is a stronger-field ligand. The new complex therefore has a LARGER Δ.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Larger Δ means a higher-energy photon is absorbed. Absorption shifts from red (low energy) toward orange-yellow (higher energy). The complementary colour seen shifts from pale blue toward deep blue/violet.`}],ans:`NH₃ replaces 4 H₂O around Cu²⁺, forming [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺. NH₃ is a stronger-field ligand than H₂O → larger Δ → higher-energy photons absorbed (orange-yellow) → complementary colour (deep blue) seen.`}],tutorPrompts:[`Why is Sc not a true transition element?`,`Explain why [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ is deeper blue than [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺`,`Walk me through why CO is toxic using transition-metal chemistry`,`Predict high-spin vs low-spin for Fe²⁺ with CN⁻ ligands, and explain your reasoning`,`Give me a challenging past-paper question on ligand exchange and stability constants`,`What's the #1 mistake students make on this topic?`]};export{e as TRANSITION};